 If two AI pricing agents can talk privately, can an overseer who sees only prices and public messages tell? We ran repeated four-seller pricing markets in which, in some games, one pair of LLM sellers got a private chat channel. Sellers were never told to collude. We compared a price-level screen, a test for hidden co-movement (conditional mutual information, pCMI), and an LLM auditor reading public messages.
 
-On scripted colluders, pCMI flags every planted pair and removes most false alarms from shared shocks, but cannot tell colluders from two seed-sharing copies of one policy. Channel games priced higher in all nine main seller sets (seven models, one prompt family; only two of five wordings resolved an effect). A price-level screen separated channel from no-channel games, while pCMI stayed near chance (0.51 in a 192-game grid). Adding public messages gave the LLM auditor no resolved gain (−0.01 [−0.09, +0.06] AUROC on a locked test). On a different, external task where the collusive act is a verbal verdict, text predicted rule-breaking well (0.87). The price signal is not specific to collusion. A private notepad with no partner produced about two-thirds of the channel premium by point estimate. One model priced high without any channel under memory and profit framing. Removing safety training did not detectably raise prices.
